# Worker Rules

## Purpose

The worker performs long-running and CPU-intensive PDF operations.

Never move heavy conversion processing into the Next.js application or Hono API.

## Stack

Use:

- Node.js
- TypeScript
- BullMQ
- Redis
- AWS S3
- Drizzle
- Neon
- Docker

Conversion engines may include:

- qpdf
- pdf-lib
- MuPDF
- Ghostscript
- LibreOffice
- Tesseract
- OCRmyPDF

## Job Flow

Preferred flow:

BullMQ Job
↓
Validate Payload
↓
Load Conversion
↓
Verify State
↓
Download Input from S3
↓
Process Locally
↓
Upload Output to S3
↓
Update Database
↓
Record Usage
↓
Cleanup Temporary Files

## Queue Payloads

Do not put binary file contents inside Redis.

Queue payloads should contain IDs and object keys.

Example:

{
conversionId,
inputObjectKey
}

Keep queue payloads small.

## Idempotency

Jobs may be delivered more than once.

Workers must be safe against duplicate execution.

Before processing:

- Check conversion state.
- Verify whether output already exists when appropriate.
- Avoid overwriting valid completed results unnecessarily.

## Retries

Differentiate between:

- Transient errors
- Permanent conversion errors

Retry transient failures.

Do not retry invalid files indefinitely.

Do not create infinite retry loops.

## Temporary Files

Worker temporary files must be isolated.

Use controlled temporary directories.

Always clean up temporary files after processing.

Cleanup must happen on:

- Success
- Failure
- Cancellation when supported

## File Security

User files are untrusted.

Never:

- Execute arbitrary user commands.
- Accept arbitrary executable paths.
- Accept arbitrary filesystem paths.
- Build shell commands from unsanitized input.

Prefer argument arrays over shell command strings.

Validate conversion options before executing native tools.

## Conversion Engines

Keep engine-specific implementations isolated.

Example:

converters/
├── qpdf/
├── ghostscript/
├── libreoffice/
├── mupdf/
└── ocr/

The conversion registry should determine which engine handles a conversion.

Do not scatter engine selection logic across the worker.

## Database State

Conversion state should be explicit.

Example:

created
queued
processing
completed
failed

Do not silently change state.

Failed jobs should preserve useful failure metadata without exposing sensitive internals to users.

## Usage

Usage must be recorded durably in PostgreSQL.

Do not use Redis as the permanent usage ledger.

Usage recording must account for retries and duplicate jobs.

## S3

Use S3 for input and output objects.

Never store binary files in PostgreSQL.

Never put binary files in Redis.

Use controlled object keys.

## Scaling

Workers must be horizontally scalable.

Do not depend on:

- Global memory
- Local process state
- A single worker instance

Durable state belongs in PostgreSQL/S3.

Queue state belongs in Redis/BullMQ.

## Docker

The worker must run inside Docker.

Keep the image deterministic.

Install only required system dependencies.

Run with the least privileges possible.

Do not depend on a specific hosting provider.

The worker should be deployable to Railway or another container platform.
